* docker is a platforme for building, running and shipping
  applications.
* is a tool for running applications in an isolated environment, similar 
   to virtual machine  
* if the application works on your machine it can run and function
  the same way on other machines


=====> Probleme: it works on my machine.
* Reasons:
    1- one or more files missing
    2- software version mismatch
    3- diffirent configuration settings.


===> for that docker comes to the rescue, with docker we can
     easily package up our application with everything it needs
     and run it anywhere on any machine with docker.

====> Image: * is a template for creating an environment.
             * it's a snapchot.
             * Has everything need to run your Apps.
             * OS, Software, App Code.

====> container: * running instance of an Image.


====> exposing port : mapping port from host to container 
* example :  we habe a single container which is running nginx, this
             container is exposing a tcp port is 80.
    we want from the host to issue a request to out container which is exposing port 80
    so we want to be able to go to our web browser type localhost 8080 and then that port
    8080 should be mapped to port 80 on the conatiner so basically we want to go 
    from the host to the conatiner
    for that by adding -p before we run the conatiner.
    -p 8080:80
  ===> exposing multiple ports:
    docker run -d -p 3000:80 -p 8080:80 nginx:latest

======> managing containers:
* starting a container:
  docker run -d -p 3000:80 -p 8080:80 nginx:latest
* list all containers running
  docker ps 
  ==> not running docker ps -a 
* stop a container:
  docker stop conID/conName
* start a container:
  docker start conID/conName
* delete a container:
  docker rm -f $(docker ps -aq) force rm all containers where they are running
  docker rm conID/conName
* naming container:
  docker run --name website -d -p 3000:80 -p 8080:80 nginx:latest

======> Container vs virtual machine
* container: is an isolated environment for running an application
    * allow running multiple apps in isolation
    * are lightweight : don't need a full operating system.
    * all containers in a single machine share the operating system of the host
    * start quickly
    * Need less hardware resources


* virtual machine: is an abstraction of a machine (physical hardware).
    Problems:
        - each VM needs a full blown OS.
        - slow to start

* heypervisor is a software we use to create and manage virtual machines
    example: VirtualBox, VMware : which are cross platform 

======> docker Architecture:
* docker uses a client server architecture so it has a client component that
  talk to a server component using a restful API, the server called the Docker Engine
  sits on the background and takes care of building and running docker containers, 
  technically a container is just a process like other processes running on your
  computer.
* all containers on a host share the operating system of the host, all these containers
  share the kernelof the host.
    ==> A kernel is the core of the OS it's like the engine of a car.
        it's the part that manages applications and hardware resources like (CPU, memory).



=======> installing docker
* A Dockerfile is a plain text file that includes instructions that docker
  uses to package up the application into an image.
  ==> this image contains everything our application needs to run.
  ==> once we have an image we tell docker to start a container using 
      that image, one we have an image we can push it to a docker registry(docker hup), 
      then we can pull it on any machines running docker 


=======> Docker Volumes:
    * allows us to share data (Files, Folders).
    * Between Host and Container.
    * Between conatiners
    docker exec -it website bash
    ==> Volumes(Between containers) : copy content from website to website_copy
    docker run --name website-copy --volumes-from website -d -p 8081:80 nginx:latest

===> Dockerfile :
  * Build own images.
  * allows us to create our own images by creating a file called 
    Dockerfile
  * this file contains a list of steps of how to create images
  * if we are building our own image we don't need to mount a volume
    -v $(pwd):/usr/share/nginx/html

==> building images:
  * we create a Dockerfile
  * docker build --tag/-t nameImage:latest URL(where the dockerfile)
  * when we have a image now we run a container from it
    docker run --name website -p 8080:80 -d website:latest





==> when we do :
  docker pull nginx:1.23
  --> Docker client will contact Docker Hub and it will say
      I want to grab the nginx image with this specific tag
      and download it locally



=====> how do we access to a container?
* application inside container runs in an isolated Docker network.
  for that we want to expose the containr port to the
  host(the machine the container runs on).
* Port binding:  bind the conatiner's port to the host's port to
  mak the service available to the outside world.
* for example : nginx port 80
  bind that container Port 80 to our local host on any port
  like 8080, so that I can access the containe or whatever is 
  running inside the conatiner as it if running on my local host
  port 8080 ===> -p 8080:80
-d ==>means in the background in detached mode
 for standard to use the same port on your host as container is using
* list of all networks that we have 
 docker netwrok ls
* creating a network:
  docker network create nameNetwork
* connecting a container to the network:
  docker  network  connect  netwrokdName/idNetwork  containerName
* running container and provide or specify a network that we want to use:
  docker  run -p 8080:80 --net  nameNetwork  containerName


 ==============> Structure of Dockefile:
 * Dockerfiles start from a parent image or base-image
 * it's a Docker image that your image is based on 
 * from, copy
 * WORKDIR: sets the working directory for all following commands
    like changing into a directory : "cd ..."

====> from docker to container:
  Dockerfile, build, Image, run ==> container.


=====> A Docker image consists of layers
* each instruction in the dockerfile creates one laayer




